scheme: U:  UCLA wins its first game.     S:  USC wins its first game.     Y:  Cal wins its first game
answer-keys: 2\.056

problem: Symb 2.057
node: ?:Assuming that either logic is difficult or the text is not readable, Alfred will pass only if he concentrates.
scheme: P:  Alfred will pass.     Q:  Alfred concentrates.     R:  logic is difficult.     S:  Alfred will secure employment.     T:  the text is readable.     X:  the lectures are exciting
answer-keys: 2\.057

problem: Symb 2.058
node: ?:If neither Richard nor Tony will pass, Yiannis won't either.
scheme: R:  Richard will pass.     T:  Tony will pass.     Y:  Yiannis will pass
answer-keys: 2\.058
note: Hint: Remember, from the Examples file, that we regard the standard symbolization of "neither P nor Q" to be "~(PvQ)".

problem: Symb 2.059
node: ?:If USC doesn't win its first game, then both Cal and UCLA won't win their first games.
scheme: U:  UCLA wins its first game.     S:  USC wins its first game.     Y:  Cal wins its first game
answer-keys: 2\.059

problem: Symb 2.060
node: ?:If either Tony or Yiannis will pass, then so will Richard unless he is tired.
scheme: "R:  Richard will pass.     T:  Tony will pass.     Y:  Yiannis will pass.     U:  Richard is tired.     "
answer-keys: 2\.060

problem: Symb 2.061
node: ?:Either Cal wins its first game and UCLA does not win its first game or if UCLA wins its first game then USC does not win its first game.
scheme: U:  UCLA wins its first game.     S:  USC wins its first game.     Y:  Cal wins its first game
answer-keys: 2\.061

problem: Symb 2.062
node: ?:Yiannis and Tony will definitely pass; Richard however, will fail if he doesn't study (and, for that matter, will fail even if he does).
scheme: R:  Richard will pass.     T:  Tony will pass.     Y:  Yiannis will pass.     V:  Richard will study.     X:  Richard will fail
answer-keys: 2\.062

problem: Symb 2.063
node: ?:If Ruth will pass so long as she isn't tired, then she'll surely pass if she studies hard.
scheme: P:  Ruth will pass.     S:  Ruth studies hard.     T:  Ruth is tired
answer-keys: 2\.063

problem: Symb 2.064
node: ?:USC wins its first game provided that UCLA does not win its first game, but USC wins its first game only if Cal wins its first game.
scheme: U:  UCLA wins its first game.     S:  USC wins its first game.     Y:  Cal wins its first game
answer-keys: 2\.064

problem: Symb 2.065
node: ?:Either it is not the case that Richard pays attention and does not lose track of the argument, or it is not the case that he does not take notes or fails to do well in the course.
scheme: P:  Richard pays attention.     S:  Richard loses track of the argument.     T:  Richard takes notes.     W:  Richard does well in the course
answer-keys: 2\.065

problem: Symb 2.066
node: ?:Given that Cal wins its first game, USC does not win its first game provided that UCLA wins its first game.
scheme: U:  UCLA wins its first game.     S:  USC wins its first game.     Y:  Cal wins its first game
answer-keys: 2\.066

problem: Symb 2.067
node: ?:If Rudolf's prayers are answered if he believes in God, then God exists.
scheme: P:  Rudolf's prayers are answered.     V:  Rudolf believes in God.     X:  God exists.     W:  this is the best of all possible worlds.     T:  everything is permitted
answer-keys: 2\.067

problem: Symb 2.068
node: ?:Rudolf does not believe in God unless this is the best of all possible worlds.
scheme: P:  Rudolf's prayers are answered.     V:  Rudolf believes in God.     X:  God exists.     W:  this is the best of all possible worlds.     T:  everything is permitted
answer-keys: 2\.068

problem: Symb 2.069
node: ?:This is not the best of all possible worlds.
scheme: P:  Rudolf's prayers are answered.     V:  Rudolf believes in God.     X:  God exists.     W:  this is the best of all possible worlds.     T:  everything is permitted
answer-keys: 2\.069

problem: Symb 2.070
node: ?:If God does not exist, then everything is permitted.
scheme: P:  Rudolf's prayers are answered.     V:  Rudolf believes in God.     X:  God exists.     W:  this is the best of all possible worlds.     T:  everything is permitted
answer-keys: 2\.070
note: Hint: With the correct symbolization, 2.070 should be derivable from 2.067-2.069

problem: Symb 2.071
node: ?:Neither UCLA nor USC will win its first game unless both are willing to work harder.
scheme: U:  UCLA wins its first game.     S:  USC wins its first game.     Y:  Cal wins its first game.     W:  UCLA is willing to work harder.     R:  USC willing to work harder
answer-keys: 2\.071

problem: Symb 2.072
node: ?:They (Richard, Tony, and Yiannis) won't all pass.
scheme: R:  Richard will pass.     T:  Tony will pass.     Y:  Yiannis will pass
answer-keys: 2\.072
note: Hint: Remember, from the Examples file, that we have adopted a convention whereby the standard symbolization of a series, such as "P and Q and R and S" is to be associated to the left.

problem: Symb 2.073
node: ?:Richard won't pass, but at least one of the other two (Tony and Yiannis) will.
scheme: R:  Richard will pass.     T:  Tony will pass.     Y:  Yiannis will pass
answer-keys: 2\.073

problem: Symb 2.076
node: ?:Unless either neither Sy nor Nye neigh or Bob and Babs both bray, none of them will beat Whirlaway.
