answer-keys: 2\.079.2\.079b.2\.079c

problem: Symb 2.080
node: ?:C.C. will pass unless the test is both too long and too hard, provided that the instructor is neither biased nor perverse.
scheme: P:  C\.C\. will pass.     T:  the test is too long.     R:  the test is too hard.     U:  the instructor is biased.     V:  the instructor is perverse
answer-keys: 2\.080

problem: Symb 2.081
node: ?:If C.C. studies logic, then he does not do well in the course only if he does not take notes and pay attention.
scheme: X:  C\.C\. studies logic.     P:  C\.C\. pays attention.     S:  C\.C\. loses track of the argument.     T:  C\.C\. takes notes.     W:  C\.C\. does well in the course
answer-keys: 2\.081

problem: Symb 2.082
node: ?:Donald is a lover of logic only if he does not organize his time.
scheme: V:  Donald is a lover of logic.     R:  Donald organizes his time.     W:  Donald enjoys whiskey at night.     Z:  Donald enjoys Mozart in the morning
answer-keys: 2\.082

problem: Symb 2.083
node: ?:Donald enjoys whiskey at night provided that he both enjoys Mozart in the morning and organizes his time.
scheme: V:  Donald is a lover of logic.     R:  Donald organizes his time.     W:  Donald enjoys whiskey at night.     Z:  Donald enjoys Mozart in the morning
answer-keys: 2\.083

problem: Symb 2.084
node: ?:If Donald is a lover of logic who organizes his time, then he enjoys Mozart in the morning or whiskey at night, but not both.
scheme: V:  Donald is a lover of logic.     R:  Donald organizes his time.     W:  Donald enjoys whiskey at night.     Z:  Donald enjoys Mozart in the morning
answer-keys: 2\.084

problem: Symb 2.085
node: ?:If Donald enjoys whiskey at night, then either he enjoys Mozart in the morning and organizes his time, or he does not enjoy Mozart in the morning and does not organize his time, or else he is not a lover of logic.
scheme: V:  Donald is a lover of logic.     R:  Donald organizes his time.     W:  Donald enjoys whiskey at night.     Z:  Donald enjoys Mozart in the morning
answer-keys: 2\.085
note: Hint: Remember, from the Examples file, that we have adopted a convention whereby the standard symbolization of a series, such as "P or Q or R or S" is to be associated to the left.

problem: Symb 2.086
node: ?:Assuming that the professor is a Communist, he will sign the loyalty oath; but if he is an idealist, he will neither sign the loyalty oath nor speak to those who do.
scheme: P:  the professor is a Communist.     S:  the professor will sign the loyalty oath.     T:  the professor is an idealist.     Y:  the professor will speak to those who do sign the loyalty oath
answer-keys: 2\.086

problem: Symb 2.087
node: ?:Errors will decrease in the subject's performance just in case neither motivation is absent nor learning has not occurred.
scheme: R:  Errors will decrease in the subject's performance.     T:  motivation is absent.     U:  learning has occurred
answer-keys: 2\.087

problem: Symb 2.088
node: ?:If Bertie and Hans are playing dice together, it is the first throw of the game, and Bertie is throwing the dice, then he wins the game on the first throw if and only if he throws 7 or 11.
